---
title: Blog Planning, Behind The Scenes
date: 2024-07-29
---

I've never been very good at keeping a journal. I'd write in them for a few days, forget about it for a day or two, and then it was like POOF! it never existed in my brain.

This blog is much the same, which is why the dates are so sporadic. I love to write. I hate to write on a schedule.

Today, I found a rather adventurous list, simply titled “blog post ideas 2”. I have no idea where version one went, or if it even existed. I don't remember. The memory of writing version 2 is vague, brought on only by reading all 12 pages of blog ideas.

That's right. At some point I had wrote 12 pages of ideas, and then blanked it from my memory.

This is not the first time I have found a 10+ page document I have wrote once upon a time, and then completely forgot about it. Deep in the recesses of my google storage and Dropbox lies so many documents half completed, so many stories half told, you might as well call it Silver's Writing Graveyard TM.

Lucky, this 12 page baby was still on the hard drive, right there in my documents folder. Overlooked and underappreciated, it sat silently for years, biding time. Today was the lucky day. I just spent 8 hours going through every idea, putting it in a category, and expanding on it where I could.

At the end of 8 hours I now have …(drumroll)… 19 pages of blog ideas! Some can't be written until certain criteria are met, some are a long way off even. Some, I don't know if I'll ever write (looking at you sheep group). But most I already have ideas for and a goal in mind.

Preparation is key, to most things. The work around the farm absolutely benefits from adequate preparation. Even if that prep is done months in advance, don't skimp on the preparation. Research being the first, the most important, and often last thing done with new folk (folk being new to… well, almost anything really). Don't be afraid to ask questions, even dumb ones. Embarrassment will not kill you, but if you don't ask the question, you might end up killing something out of ignorance. The stakes are high when you are a farmer or homesteader.

Research is the absolute best and first thing you could ever do. Learn to vet sources, and not believe the first (or even third) thing you read. Proper research is a learned skill, not an inherent one.

We, as farmers, literally have lives in our hands everyday. Don't you think that warrants a bit of reading?